2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"In 2019 the marginal propensity to consume in the UK was estimated to be 0.8. Define the term ‘marginal propensity to consume’.","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["marginal propensity to consume","definition"],"qpPage":4,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-as-p2-2024-jun-q2c","p":42,"section":"A","questionNumber":"2(c)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"In 2019 the marginal propensity to consume in the UK was estimated to be 0.8. Using the above data, which one of the following represents the value of the multiplier?","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","marginal propensity to consume","calculation"],"qpPage":4,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["0.2","0.8","1.3","5.0"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"edexcel-a-p2-2024-jun-q4b","p":14,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(b)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"In July 2020 a survey estimated that the marginal propensity to consume in the UK was 0.1. Calculate the total increase in aggregate demand from an increase in government spending of £60 million.","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","marginal propensity to consume","government spending","calculation"],"qpPage":9,"msPage":12,"ctxPage":8,"modelAnswer":null,"ctxLabel":"Question 4 as printed"},{"id":"edexcel-a-p2-2023-jun-q4b","p":13,"section":"A","questionNumber":"4(b)","parentQuestion":"4","choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"UK consumption, % of GDP, 2004–2021 With reference to the chart on the previous page, explain the likely effect of a fall in the marginal propensity to consume on the level of real GDP. Refer to the multiplier in your answer.","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier","2-2-2-consumption"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","marginal propensity to consume","real GDP"],"qpPage":9,"msPage":8,"ctxPage":8,"modelAnswer":null,"ctxLabel":"Question 4 as printed"},{"id":"edexcel-a-p2-2022-jun-q1a","p":12,"section":"A","questionNumber":"1(a)","parentQuestion":"1","choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"According to the Bank of England, the UK’s marginal propensity to consume is estimated to be 0.64. Using the information above, calculate the total reduction in the UK’s GDP from a fall in government spending of £20 million.","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","marginal propensity to consume","government spending","calculation"],"qpPage":2,"msPage":4,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p2-2021-nov-q1b","p":16,"section":"A","questionNumber":"1(b)","parentQuestion":"1","choiceGroup":null,"marks":2,"questionText":"After the Global Financial Crisis of 2008, the US President introduced expansionary fiscal policies of $800 billion. The International Monetary Fund estimated that the multiplier at the time was approximately 1.5. Calculate the total final increase in US aggregate demand as a result of the President’s ‘expansionary fiscal policies’, assuming no other changes.","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","fiscal policy","aggregate demand","calculation"],"qpPage":2,"msPage":4,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p2-2020-oct-q2a","p":17,"section":"A","questionNumber":"2(a)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"The planned investment in the redevelopment of Dover Port will cost £115 million. The Chief Executive said it will lead to many ‘new job opportunities for local people and a transformed waterfront experience’ with new cafés, bars, and shops. If the value of the multiplier is 1.8, which one of the following will be the total increase in GDP from the redevelopment, assuming other things are equal?","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier","2-2-3-investment"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","investment","calculation"],"qpPage":4,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["£64 million","£115 million","£207 million","£230 million"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"edexcel-a-p2-2020-oct-q2b","p":17,"section":"A","questionNumber":"2(b)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"The planned investment in the redevelopment of Dover Port will cost £115 million. The Chief Executive said it will lead to many ‘new job opportunities for local people and a transformed waterfront experience’ with new cafés, bars, and shops. Using the example above, explain how the multiplier process leads to an increase in aggregate demand.","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier","2-2-3-investment"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","investment","aggregate demand"],"qpPage":4,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-as-p2-2019-jun-q1b","p":39,"section":"A","questionNumber":"1(b)","parentQuestion":"1","choiceGroup":null,"marks":2,"questionText":"It is estimated that building a second runway at Gatwick airport will require an investment of £9.3 billion. The total increase in UK GDP as a result of this injection is expected to be around £44.1 billion. Calculate the value of the multiplier based on the data given. You are advised to show your working.","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","calculation","Gatwick"],"qpPage":2,"msPage":4,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-as-p2-2019-jun-q1c","p":39,"section":"A","questionNumber":"1(c)","parentQuestion":"1","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"It is estimated that building a second runway at Gatwick airport will require an investment of £9.3 billion. The total increase in UK GDP as a result of this injection is expected to be around £44.1 billion. Which one of the following is a likely cause of a decrease in the value of the UK’s multiplier?","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","marginal propensity to import","marginal propensity to save"],"qpPage":3,"msPage":4,"ctxPage":2,"modelAnswer":null,"ctxLabel":"Question 1 as printed","type":"mcq","options":["A decrease in the marginal propensity to save","A decrease in the marginal propensity to tax","An increase in the marginal propensity to consume","An increase in the marginal propensity to import"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"edexcel-a-p2-2018-jun-q2a","p":10,"section":"A","questionNumber":"2(a)","parentQuestion":"2","choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"Which one of the following would be most likely to cause aggregate demand to increase? A fall in:","topics":["2-2-1-aggregate-demand","2-2-2-consumption","2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["aggregate demand","marginal propensity to save","consumption"],"qpPage":4,"msPage":5,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["government spending","net trade (X–M)","the marginal propensity to consume","the marginal propensity to save"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"edexcel-as-p2-2018-jun-q6d","p":38,"section":"B","questionNumber":"6(d)","parentQuestion":"6","choiceGroup":null,"marks":6,"questionText":"Explain two factors that might cause a rise in the value of the multiplier.","topics":["2-4-4-the-multiplier"],"board":"edexcel","groups":["theme-2"],"keywords":["multiplier","marginal propensity to consume","MPC","withdrawals"],"qpPage":15,"msPage":13,"ctxPage":14,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"edexcel-a-p2-2018-jun-q7","p":10,"section":"C","questionNumber":"7","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":"edexcel-a-p2-2018-jun-sec-c","marks":25,"questionText":"In 2016 the UK Chancellor of the Exchequer announced that the government would spend an extra £23 billion on innovation and infrastructure o